.docname {Karbone}
.docdescription {A modern Kotlin SDK for the Carbone.io document generation API.}
.htmloptions baseurl:{https://ekino.github.io/Karbone}
.include {docs}

.html
    <p style="margin: 0 0 1.5rem 0"><img src="karbone-mark.svg" alt="Karbone" width="96" height="96"></p>

Karbone is a Kotlin SDK for the [Carbone.io](https://carbone.io/) document generation API.

It is meant as a modern, idiomatic and complete replacement for the official
[carbone-sdk-java](https://github.com/carboneio/carbone-sdk-java), which has been left incomplete and dated: no coroutine support,
no typed render options, string-typed errors, and only partial coverage of the API.

> Karbone is in early development. The API is not stable yet.

## Why Karbone?

- **Typed everywhere.** `RenderOptions`, `OutputFormat` and its `PdfOptions` / `ImageOptions` / `CsvOptions`, `UploadOptions`,
  `TemplatePatch`... all the fields Carbone accepts are Kotlin types with builders, not raw JSON maps.
- **Errors as values.** Every call is `context(_: Raise<KarboneError>) suspend fun ...`, built on [Arrow](https://arrow-kt.io/)'s
  `Raise`. No exceptions to catch, no silent nulls; `KarboneError` is a sealed hierarchy you can exhaustively `when` on. A
  synchronous, exception-throwing facade is available for Java and other non-coroutine callers.
  See [Errors](errors.qd).
- **Coroutine-first.** Every network call is a `suspend fun`; pagination is exposed as a `Flow`.
- **Hash-first rendering.** Rendering from local bytes never requires a separate upload step: Karbone addresses the template by
  its SHA-256 and uploads on demand only if Carbone does not know it yet. See [Rendering](rendering.qd).
- **On-premise first-class.** Cloud and self-hosted `carbone-ee` are both first-class targets, with the differences (API
  versioning header, authentication, template management) handled explicitly rather than assumed away. See
  [On-premise](on-premise.qd).
- **Zero HTTP dependency.** Built on the JDK's own `java.net.http.HttpClient`, `kotlinx.serialization` for JSON, and
  `arrow-core` for errors — no OkHttp, Retrofit or Ktor client pulled into your dependency graph.

## Where to go next

- [Quickstart](quickstart.qd) — install Karbone and render your first document.
- [Configuration](configuration.qd) — `KarboneConfig`, timeouts, retries, logging.
- [Rendering](rendering.qd) — template sources, render data, render options, sync vs async.
- [Templates](templates.qd) — upload, download, update, delete, list, categories and tags.
- [Errors](errors.qd) — the `KarboneError` hierarchy and how to handle it.
- [On-premise](on-premise.qd) — running and authenticating against a self-hosted `carbone-ee`.
- [Testing](testing.qd) — testing code that depends on Karbone with `FakeKarbone`.
